00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone i'm john coogan and welcome  to the fourth installment of my guide to  startup fundraising  last week i talked about how to meet  investors and today i'm going to talk  about building a great deck  remember building a great deck doesn't  mean you've built a great business  you need to make sure your company is  making its customers happy growing the  customer base significantly every month  and has a giant pool of potential  customers if you've done all that  you might not even need a deck to close  a big round decks come into play when a  professional investor  is ready to make a decision on whether  or not to invest most  angel investors never need to see a deck  to make a decision they just want to  talk to you directly and hear about the  business  but for professional investors like seed  funds and vc firms  you'll need to pitch a larger audience  and have some materials that can be  circulated within the firm  it can be intimidating to think about  all the different people that will judge  you based on this deck  but don't let that stop you from keeping  it simple stay concise  eight to ten slides are perfect for a  seed round you might expand this to  15 or 20 slides for a series a but the  information always needs to be laid out  in a clean and simple manner  remember after investors sign a term  sheet they can request  more specific information during due  diligence which we'll talk about in a  later video  never waste money buying complicated  templates or paying for design services  i've linked two great slide deck  templates from y combinator and sequoia  capital below  they might seem overly simplistic but  just look at any apple keynote and  you'll see how effective slides can be  when they keep things straightforward  during a pitch the deck shouldn't be the  main attraction vcs invest in people  not decks so you want to be able to have  a great conversation and just use the  slides to guide things  focus on short bullet points and key  takeaways instead of full sentences or  paragraphs  a great slide deck will cover the  following 10 items your company's  purpose  the problem the solution the why now the  market size  the competition your product your  business model the founding team  and your financials and traction let's  go through them one by one  we talked about the elevator pitch  before and your company's purpose slide  is a great opportunity to reiterate that  this description isn't just for the  investor you're currently meeting with  it's also the phrase that they'll repeat  internally at their firm  and potentially send to other investors  who they collaborate with  make it clear make it concise and make  it memorable  substack did a great job of this with  their first major deck when substack  launched in 2018  patreon had already raised over 100  million dollars and most investors were  familiar with that model  but it hadn't been widely adopted by  newsletter writers additionally ben  thompson had proved that independent  writers could gain lots of subscribed  followers with his newsletter  clearly describing who the customer is  and what you do for them  is critical to keeping this slide  memorable describing the problem in the  solution is the best way to introduce  investors to your market opportunity  in talking about the problem make sure  to describe who the customer is  what their pain is or what they don't  have the ability to do today  and then talk about how they solve this  problem currently  the solution slide is where you  demonstrate your company's value  proposition  and how you solve the customer's problem  this is a great place to show how your  customer uses your product and what  value they get out of it  this format works for all kinds of  startups but airbnb has a particularly  good set of slides on the problem and  the solution  they highlight three common problems  when traveling the high price of current  options  the lack of community when staying in a  hotel and the fact that people with  extra space can't easily host travelers  to make extra money  their solution is a web platform that  lets users rent out their space to host  travelers  in doing so airbnb users can save money  while traveling  make money while hosting and connect to  the city  after you've articulated the problem in  the solution you'll need to explain why  this is the best time to start a company  focused on providing a solution  timing is super important in startups if  a particular technology is not quite  polished enough  or affordable yet you could be too soon  to gain wide adoption  conversely if you enter the market too  late  there will likely be plenty of  competitors providing a better solution  already  the social media management company  buffer did a great job of explaining the  growth of social media  in 2010 when they launched they showed  that social media was basically  doubling every year and they make the  case that soon  every business would need to manage  social media pages buffers products are  designed to make this easier  so even though the need might have been  small back in 2010  it was the right time to focus on  building tools for social media  management  it's not enough to just be building  something in a growing market investors  have to believe that the size of your  market will eventually be huge  this slide serves to profile who you  will ultimately generate revenue from  and how big the opportunity is you can  think about this in three ways  the total available market or tam which  is a very broad  top-down approach that captures all of  the revenue in a given market  then there's the serviceable available  market which is the part of the tam  targeted to your products and services  specifically  lastly there's the serviceable  obtainable market or som  which is the part of the market that you  can actually capture you want to strike  a balance here and come up with a number  that isn't so huge that you'll get  laughed at  but isn't so small that you've left out  obvious adjacent markets that you could  expand into  linkedin did a great job of sizing their  market in 2004 when they raised their  series b  they highlighted three key areas where  they could monetize the service  and offered comparable products and  market sizes for each  remarkably their total market size was  just over 10 billion dollars in this  deck  and in 2020 linkedin is estimated to  earn over 8 billion  in revenue not bad for a 16 year old  prediction  the competition slide can be a tough one  because entrepreneurs often don't like  admitting that there are other people  working on the same thing it makes their  ideas seem less  novel but it's a fact of life if you're  going after a big problem there will be  other companies offering solutions  already  you want to make sure that you're not  just listing competitors though you want  to show how you're different  it's common practice to use a 2x2 matrix  to illustrate your market position  front app did a great job of this in  their series a deck the company makes a  shared inbox so multiple people can  collaborate on emails together  typically options for this fell into two  separate categories  business ready and good messaging  experience and there wasn't much  overlap front was aiming to create  business software that would feel as  intuitive to use as consumer apps like  gmail and facebook messenger  this slide clearly shows how they stand  out from the competition  now that the customer and market are  clear in the investor's mind  you need to focus on your product and  solutions specifically  you need to break down your form factor  functionality features and intellectual  properties so everyone in the meeting  understands what you're building  investors try out lots of startups but  certain products  especially in enterprise and biotech  might not be immediately relevant to an  investor  so go into enough detail here to make it  easy to visualize your product  front app did a great job of explaining  their product in their fundraising deck  because it's a bit of a complicated  enterprise product with lots of features  they use two slides to illustrate the  design first  they show you a basic screenshot that  gives you a look at the app as the  customer would see it  then they flip to a second slide with  call outs for all the different features  tucking those advanced features into a  friendly design is their main value  proposition  so it's important to call out these in  the deck your business model is how you  plan to make money from all those  customers who love using your product  you should explain your pricing  structure how much you typically earn  from a customer  and how you plan on acquiring new  customers twine.fm  is a platform for freelancers who create  content for brands  their business model slide is extremely  straightforward they take a 20  commission on all transactions that  happen on their platform  sometimes you may need to explain your  plans in more detail like foursquare did  in their 2011 pitch deck  since foursquare was a social network it  was free to use for the end user  and they relied on monetization from  local businesses foursquare's initial  business model didn't work well though  and they had to pivot so be sure you're  really testing your assumptions here  the team at the earliest stages this is  the most important slide of the deck  investors will often make bets on  talented founders even though they  aren't sure that the current business  will work out  you need to show that you have the  experience and determination to make  your startup successful no matter what  investors want to know that there is a  committed team on board with the  relevant skills to build and manage the  company as it grows  square's first pitch deck is a great  example of how to highlight your team  the founders were very well known in  silicon valley for their previous work  and it was clear that they could manage  a high-growth tech company today  square is worth over 60 billion dollars  companies that aren't growing  can't really call themselves startups  they're just small businesses  showing big growth numbers is going to  be the data that proves the hypothesis  you've laid out in the rest of the deck  you've explained how the business works  but in order to merit investment you  have to be growing  and the faster the better the analytics  company mixpanel  outlined their growth very clearly in  this fundraising deck  the exact figures have been redacted but  you can see that the growth curve is  up and to the right they also include a  sample summary of their financing to  date  which helps investors understand where  the company is and what the next round  might look like  linkedin also demonstrated a great  technique for showing growth  they started by showing what their  initial growth projections were when  they raised their series a  and then on the next slide they show  what actually happened they outperformed  significantly  and it's really clear from the slide  that they're onto something real  decks are just one small piece of the  fundraising puzzle and in many cases you  might not need a deck at all  so don't spend too much time building it  remember that they're just a tool to  structure your thoughts  and are never really a replacement for  an engaging conversation  ideally the deck will drift into the  background as you start diving into  different opportunities for where the  company could go  and how big the opportunity is next week  i'll talk about how to read a term sheet  and close a deal  so hit the subscribe button and ring the  bell if you want to be notified when  that goes live  if you have any questions about decks or  think i missed something please leave me  a comment below  thanks for watching

